  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Editorial standards</p>
    <h1>The standard this site is written to</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Tan Lines is an independent publication. Every article here is written to
      one editorial standard, and this page is that standard, set down in the
      open so any reader &mdash; or any regulator, dermatologist, or skeptical
      journalist &mdash; can hold the work to it.
    </p>

    <h2>Who writes this</h2>
    <p>
      Tan Lines is written and edited under a single masthead rather than under
      individual bylines. The name on every article is the publication&rsquo;s,
      and the standard on this page is the author of record. We claim no medical
      or clinical credentials, and we never borrow them: nothing here is written
      in the voice of a doctor giving advice, because it is not.
    </p>
    <p>
      We are independent of the industry we cover. Tan Lines is not affiliated
      with, funded by, or speaking for any manufacturer, vendor, clinic, or any
      of the companies whose drugs appear in these pages. It sells nothing.
    </p>

    <h2>How we report</h2>
    <p>
      Every article is built from primary sources &mdash; peer-reviewed
      literature, patent filings, regulatory records, and companies&rsquo; own
      disclosures &mdash; and the specific claims are checked against those
      sources before anything is published. Dates, names, and numbers are meant
      to be verifiable; where a patent, trial, or approval is cited, it is a
      real one you can look up. The citations gathered across The Record are
      listed on the <a href="/sources/">sources page</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every article is dated. Where the evidence is thin, the article says so.
      Where a popular claim is wrong, the article says that too &mdash; including
      claims made by people selling things.
    </p>

    <h2>What we never publish</h2>
    <p>
      This site covers dosing and sourcing &mdash; as attributed record,
      never as advice. Every number is tied to a paper, a regulator, or
      explicitly to what the forums say; nothing is presented as this
      site&rsquo;s own recommendation, because it is not a dosing
      authority and no regulator has ever approved a dose. It sells
      nothing: no vendor is named or linked, no seller is ranked, no
      position is taken on where to buy. No medical advice. Melanotan II
      is not approved by any regulator for any use, and this site treats
      it as the cautionary tale it is, not a manual for it.
    </p>

    <h2>Where we&rsquo;re opinionated, and say so</h2>
    <p>
      Tan Lines holds one openly stated position: that the world still needs
      what the original researchers were chasing &mdash; a rigorously tested,
      approved medicine that gives skin durable protection from ultraviolet
      damage. When we argue that case, we label it as what it is: hope, clearly
      marked, kept separate from the reporting. The reasoning behind it lives on
      the <a href="/about/">about page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      When we find an error, or one is brought to our attention, we fix it and
      note the date of any material change. Freshness is only signalled when the
      substance of an article actually changed &mdash; we do not restamp dates to
      look current. An accurate record is worth more than a fresh-looking one.
    </p>
    <ol class="correction-log">
      <li>
        <strong>22 November 2026 &mdash; the Schedule 9 claim, removed.</strong>
        Several articles and the regulatory tracker claimed Australia moved
        Melanotan II to Schedule 9 of the Poisons Standard in February 2026,
        making possession itself an offence. No such reclassification exists on
        the record: melanotan II has been listed in Schedule 4 &mdash;
        prescription-only &mdash; since the scheduling delegate's final decision
        of April 2018, both 2026 editions of the Standard still list it there,
        and no Melanotan product has ever been on the ARTG. Corrected in
        <a href="/articles/what-the-warnings-actually-did/">what the warnings
        actually did</a>, <a href="/articles/is-melanotan-legal/">is melanotan
        legal?</a>, <a href="/articles/safety-by-numbers/">has anyone died from
        melanotan II?</a>, <a href="/articles/not-melatonin/">melatonin vs
        melanotan</a>, <a href="/articles/the-natural-fallacy/">is melanotan II
        natural?</a>, <a href="/articles/the-prevention-claim-misread/">the
        prevention claim, misread</a>, and on the
        <a href="/regulatory/">tracker</a>. See the
        <a href="/record/2018-04-10-melanotan-ii-schedule-4/">record entry</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>22 November 2026 &mdash; LegitScript's advertising figure.</strong>
        <a href="/articles/how-its-sold-now/">How it's sold now</a> reported
        melanotan-related advertising volume &ldquo;up 208%&rdquo; from 2023 to
        2024. The published dataset says 308%; the 678% (vs 2022), 276%
        marketplace and 75% social figures were already correct. See the
        <a href="/record/2025-12-09-legitscript-dataset/">record entry</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>22 November 2026 &mdash; the DHSC written answer.</strong>
        Dated 15 December 2025, answering a question tabled on 3 December
        &mdash; not 1 December 2025 as first published in <em>what the warnings
        actually did</em> and on the tracker. See the
        <a href="/record/2025-12-15-dhsc-written-answer/">record entry</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>22 November 2026 &mdash; tracker card corrections.</strong>
        The CUV105 vitiligo trial enrolled 210 patients, not 200; the EMA's
        CUV107 scientific advice was announced 24 April 2026; Tanabe's positive
        INSPIRE topline was announced 20 January 2026; an unverified February
        2026 &ldquo;HHS peptide reshuffle&rdquo; card was replaced with the
        verified 15 April 2026 FDA removals from the restricted Category 2
        list; and an unverified February 2026 Schedule 9 card was replaced with
        the freedom-of-information release of the adverse-event file (FOI
        26-3029). The April 2018 Schedule 4 listing was added as the milestone
        it is.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </article>
